1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,567
A huge transfer reaches a shared connection
just before a tiny

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:09,133
message. If the huge transfer holds the
connection until its whole

3
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:13,700
message finishes, the small one gets a very
long wait. Yet

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,267
the Internet carries enormous transfers and
tiny interactions together. How does

5
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:22,833
it divide their turns? Watch us replace one
giant uninterrupted shape

6
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:27,400
with smaller pieces. Those boundaries are not
just a drawing choice.

7
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,967
They create opportunities for other traffic to
use the same physical

8
00:00:31,967 --> 00:00:36,533
link.

9
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:41,690
Call each piece a packet. It carries a limited
portion of

10
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:46,848
the data, along with information needed to
handle that piece. The

11
00:00:46,848 --> 00:00:52,005
receiver eventually needs the right content,
but the network can now

12
00:00:52,005 --> 00:00:57,162
work with individual units instead of waiting
for one entire application

13
00:00:57,162 --> 00:01:02,319
message. Our path stays fixed. We will not
decide how that

14
00:01:02,319 --> 00:01:07,476
path was chosen or how an application recovers
missing data. Our

15
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:12,633
question is what dividing the transfer changes
about sharing and timing.

16
00:01:12,633 --> 00:01:17,312
Let blue represent the large transfer and
green the small message.

17
00:01:17,312 --> 00:01:21,992
Blue sends one packet, then green gets a turn
before another

18
00:01:21,992 --> 00:01:26,671
blue packet. The link is still transmitting
one sequence of bits.

19
00:01:26,671 --> 00:01:31,350
We did not create two simultaneous exits. What
changed is the

20
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:36,029
size of the indivisible turn: the waiting
green data no longer

21
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:40,708
needs the entire blue message to finish first.
This interleaving is

22
00:01:40,708 --> 00:01:45,387
possible sharing, not a promise that every
real queue always schedules

23
00:01:45,387 --> 00:01:50,067
these colors fairly.

24
00:01:50,067 --> 00:01:54,746
A packet is smaller than the complete
transfer, but it is

25
00:01:54,746 --> 00:01:59,425
not instantaneous. Its bits must enter the
outgoing link one after

26
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:04,104
another. Imagine the packet as a rectangle
whose width is its

27
00:02:04,104 --> 00:02:08,783
transmission time. Making a smaller rectangle
means finishing that turn sooner;

28
00:02:08,783 --> 00:02:13,462
making the link faster also makes the
rectangle narrower. These are

29
00:02:13,462 --> 00:02:18,142
two different ways to reduce the time taken by
one transmission.

30
00:02:18,142 --> 00:02:22,821
To calculate either change, we need the
packet's length and the

31
00:02:22,821 --> 00:02:27,500
link's bit rate.

32
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:32,276
Let L be the number of bits in a packet and

33
00:02:32,276 --> 00:02:37,052
R the number of bits the link can transmit
each second.

34
00:02:37,052 --> 00:02:41,829
Sending L bits takes L divided by R seconds.
Our example

35
00:02:41,829 --> 00:02:46,605
packet contains fifteen hundred bytes, or
twelve thousand bits. On a

36
00:02:46,605 --> 00:02:51,381
ten-million-bit-per-second link, that occupies
one point two milliseconds. We are calculating

37
00:02:51,381 --> 00:02:56,157
transmission time only. Signal travel adds
delay, and it is left

38
00:02:56,157 --> 00:03:00,933
out of this particular arithmetic.

39
00:03:00,933 --> 00:03:05,500
Now add an intermediate switch in our model.
It receives the

40
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:10,067
complete packet before starting to send that
packet on the next

41
00:03:10,067 --> 00:03:14,633
link. This is store-and-forward operation.
Follow the blue rectangle: first it

42
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:19,200
finishes arriving, then it begins leaving. The
switch can use other

43
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:23,767
forwarding methods in other settings, but
keeping this rule explicit lets

44
00:03:23,767 --> 00:03:28,333
us reason clearly. A packet's boundary gives
this switch a complete

45
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:32,900
unit to receive, hold briefly, and forward
toward the next fixed

46
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,467
stop.

47
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:42,033
Place three equal-rate links in a row. Our one
packet occupies

48
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:46,600
the first link for one point two milliseconds,
then the second,

49
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:51,167
then the third. Ignoring propagation,
processing, and competing traffic, its final

50
00:03:51,167 --> 00:03:55,733
bit reaches the end after three point six
milliseconds. Three links

51
00:03:55,733 --> 00:04:00,300
multiplied by one transmission time gives this
result because our single

52
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:04,867
packet must finish one store-and-forward step
before beginning the next. There

53
00:04:04,867 --> 00:04:09,433
is not yet another packet available to overlap
any of those

54
00:04:09,433 --> 00:04:14,000
steps.

55
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,733
Now prepare four packets. After the first
leaves the source, must

56
00:04:18,733 --> 00:04:23,467
the source remain idle until that packet
reaches the destination? It

57
00:04:23,467 --> 00:04:28,200
does not. The first link is free for packet
two while

58
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,933
the first packet uses link two. This is the
next opportunity

59
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:37,667
created by dividing the message. Different
links can handle different packets

60
00:04:37,667 --> 00:04:42,400
at the same time. The network becomes a
pipeline, with several

61
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:47,133
stages doing useful work during the same
interval instead of waiting

62
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:51,867
for one complete journey.

63
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:56,895
Watch the first three slots. At first, only
the first link

64
00:04:56,895 --> 00:05:01,924
works. Next, links one and two work on
different packets. Then

65
00:05:01,924 --> 00:05:06,952
all three links are occupied. Follow the
packet colors: simultaneous work

66
00:05:06,952 --> 00:05:11,981
does not mean the same packet magically
appears everywhere. Each packet

67
00:05:11,981 --> 00:05:17,010
still follows the store-and-forward rule. The
overlap happens between different packets

68
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:22,038
on different links, just as several items can
occupy different stages

69
00:05:22,038 --> 00:05:27,067
of a process without any item skipping a
stage.

70
00:05:27,067 --> 00:05:31,967
For one packet, the journey needs three
transmission slots. Once the

71
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:36,867
pipeline is filled, each additional packet can
finish one slot after

72
00:05:36,867 --> 00:05:41,767
the previous one. Four packets therefore need
three slots for the

73
00:05:41,767 --> 00:05:46,667
first, then three more for the remaining
packets: six slots total.

74
00:05:46,667 --> 00:05:51,567
At one point two milliseconds per slot, that
is seven point

75
00:05:51,567 --> 00:05:56,467
two milliseconds. Sending each packet through
the entire path before starting

76
00:05:56,467 --> 00:06:01,367
another would need twelve slots. The
improvement comes from overlap, while

77
00:06:01,367 --> 00:06:06,267
the individual links keep the same rate.

78
00:06:06,267 --> 00:06:11,105
With N equal packets and H equal-rate links,
this ideal pipeline

79
00:06:11,105 --> 00:06:15,943
takes N plus H minus one transmission slots.
Multiply that count

80
00:06:15,943 --> 00:06:20,781
by L divided by R to obtain the total time.
The

81
00:06:20,781 --> 00:06:25,619
minus one is not a mysterious correction: the
first packet is

82
00:06:25,619 --> 00:06:30,457
already included when we count the H slots
that fill its

83
00:06:30,457 --> 00:06:35,295
journey. Unequal rates, propagation,
processing, and other traffic change the
timing.

84
00:06:35,295 --> 00:06:40,133
The formula describes our clean model.

85
00:06:40,133 --> 00:06:44,758
Bring back the green message. Sources do not
always take neat

86
00:06:44,758 --> 00:06:49,383
repeating turns. Some are quiet, some send
bursts, and several can

87
00:06:49,383 --> 00:06:54,008
suddenly want the same output. Packet
switching lets a link carry

88
00:06:54,008 --> 00:06:58,633
whichever packets are selected when capacity
becomes available, instead of requiring

89
00:06:58,633 --> 00:07:03,258
each source to occupy a fixed turn
continuously. That flexibility helps

90
00:07:03,258 --> 00:07:07,883
share a connection among uneven conversations.
It also means we must

91
00:07:07,883 --> 00:07:12,508
examine what happens when several ready
packets want the same next

92
00:07:12,508 --> 00:07:17,133
transmission opportunity.

93
00:07:17,133 --> 00:07:21,812
When arrivals reach an output faster than it
can transmit, packets

94
00:07:21,812 --> 00:07:26,492
wait. Notice the green packet can still be
delayed behind blue

95
00:07:26,492 --> 00:07:31,171
packets. Packet boundaries create chances for
interleaving, but they do not

96
00:07:31,171 --> 00:07:35,850
guarantee a perfectly fair schedule, zero
waiting, or unlimited capacity. If

97
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:40,529
a finite queue fills, packets can be dropped.
Those details limit

98
00:07:40,529 --> 00:07:45,208
our opening promise: breaking data into pieces
makes flexible sharing possible,

99
00:07:45,208 --> 00:07:49,888
but the particular scheduling and available
resources determine the experience each

100
00:07:49,888 --> 00:07:54,567
packet actually gets.

101
00:07:54,567 --> 00:07:59,133
Making packets tiny seems tempting. Each turn
would finish quickly, so

102
00:07:59,133 --> 00:08:03,700
perhaps we should use the smallest pieces
imaginable. But each packet

103
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:08,267
also carries handling information, and
equipment must process those packets. More

104
00:08:08,267 --> 00:08:12,833
pieces can mean more overhead for the same
useful content. The

105
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:17,400
picture shows that trade-off by keeping the
useful blue payload separate

106
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:21,967
from the small header. Packetization is a
design choice balancing useful

107
00:08:21,967 --> 00:08:26,533
sharing opportunities, transmission
efficiency, and the work required to handle
individual

108
00:08:26,533 --> 00:08:31,100
units.

109
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:36,129
At the far end, follow the pieces belonging to
the blue

110
00:08:36,129 --> 00:08:41,157
transfer and the green message. Their journeys
shared resources, but their

111
00:08:41,157 --> 00:08:46,186
identities remain distinguishable. Recovering
the complete application meaning requires the
appropriate

112
00:08:46,186 --> 00:08:51,214
endpoint mechanisms, which sit outside our
timing model. Our network's contribution

113
00:08:51,214 --> 00:08:56,243
was to move manageable units between
successive links. It never needed

114
00:08:56,243 --> 00:09:01,271
the whole blue application message to occupy
one continuous end-to-end turn

115
00:09:01,271 --> 00:09:06,300
before allowing green data onto the same
shared infrastructure.

116
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:11,092
The tiny message escaped the elephant's full
turn because the elephant

117
00:09:11,092 --> 00:09:15,883
was divided into packets. That created shorter
opportunities for sharing. It

118
00:09:15,883 --> 00:09:20,675
also let different packets occupy successive
links at once, turning a

119
00:09:20,675 --> 00:09:25,467
chain of transmissions into a pipeline.
Queues, overhead, and finite rates

120
00:09:25,467 --> 00:09:30,258
still matter, so packet switching is not a
trick for unlimited

121
00:09:30,258 --> 00:09:35,050
speed. Its power is more practical: many
uneven conversations can share

122
00:09:35,050 --> 00:09:39,842
links in small units, while each link keeps
doing one clear

123
00:09:39,842 --> 00:09:44,633
transmission job at a time.
